---
// Lesson 3.4a — Turret Damage, Tuning & Playtest
// Closing the turret loop: Implementing damage on Projectile.cs,
// connecting collision patterns to Unit 2, tuning difficulty matrices, and partner playtesting.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>3.4a — Turret Damage, Tuning &amp; Playtest</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Your turret detects you, aims, and shoots on a timer. But currently, bullets fly harmlessly
    through your body. Today, you complete the loop by connecting projectile impacts to your
    health system, establish the four tuning knobs of enemy difficulty, and run a rigorous partner playtest.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Comparative Thinking</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Notice how our earlier lessons directly solve today's challenge:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>How is a projectile hitting the player similar to a spike?</strong> Both reduce the player's life by invoking <code>StatManager.manager.ChangeHealth(-damage)</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>How is a projectile similar to a coin?</strong> Both use trigger colliders (<code>Is Trigger = true</code>) and call <code>Destroy(gameObject)</code> upon contact.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Where Does the Hit Detection Belong?</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Should <code>OnTriggerEnter2D</code> check for hits inside <code>PlayerController.cs</code> or inside <code>Projectile.cs</code>? Why is placing the collision handler on the projectile better when your game introduces arrows, magic blasts, and boss lasers later?</li>
            <li>What happens if the projectile accidentally damages other enemies or the turret that spawned it? How does <code>other.CompareTag("Player")</code> ensure safety?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">API Documentation &amp; Isolated Patterns</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Connecting collision physics to gameplay consequences requires three distinct programming operations:
        filtering target identity, invoking external state managers, and cleaning up spent game objects.
        Study these isolated function patterns from disparate computing systems:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>API Method</th><th>Signature / Syntax</th><th>Engine Behavior</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>OnTriggerEnter2D</code></td>
            <td><code>void OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)</code></td>
            <td>Executed automatically when a trigger collider overlaps another collider in 2D space.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>CompareTag</code></td>
            <td><code>bool CompareTag(string tag)</code></td>
            <td>High-performance string tag check on the colliding object to identify its classification.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Destroy</code></td>
            <td><code>Destroy(Object obj)</code></td>
            <td>Schedules a GameObject or Component for removal from memory at the end of the active frame.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h4>Pattern 1: Tag Filtering (Airport Luggage Scanner)</h4>
    <p>
        An automated security conveyor inspects baggage tags to route hazardous materials to an isolation bay:
    </p>
    <pre><code>// Inspects luggage tag metadata before routing
void InspectLuggage(Collider2D item)
{
    if (item.CompareTag("HazardousCargo"))
    {
        RerouteToContainment();
    }
}</code></pre>

    <h4>Pattern 2: Global Service Invocation (Power Grid Overload)</h4>
    <p>
        An industrial transformer discharges a surge into a central energy grid by invoking a singleton manager:
    </p>
    <pre><code>// Modifies a global energy pool via singleton access
void DischargeToGrid(int wattsToDrain)
{
    if (PowerGridManager.instance != null)
    {
        // Passing a negative number decrements the central pool
        PowerGridManager.instance.AdjustPowerSupply(-wattsToDrain);
    }
}</code></pre>

    <h4>Pattern 3: Impact Disposal (Firework Rocket Casing)</h4>
    <p>
        A spent pyrotechnic shell destroys its own physical casing upon detonation to prevent duplicate impacts:
    </p>
    <pre><code>// Removes this GameObject immediately from the active scene hierarchy
Destroy(gameObject);</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Predict Before Building</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>If you omit <code>Destroy(gameObject);</code> from an impact callback, what happens if the moving entity remains inside the target collider for 10 consecutive frames?</li>
            <li>Notice how <code>DischargeToGrid</code> passes negative <code>-wattsToDrain</code> to <code>AdjustPowerSupply()</code>. What would happen in our game if we mistakenly passed positive <code>damage</code> to <code>ChangeHealth()</code>?</li>
            <li>Why is checking <code>if (StatManager.manager != null)</code> critical before attempting to invoke <code>ChangeHealth()</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Your Turn</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural Specification for <code>Projectile.cs</code>:</strong>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Damage Tuning Knob:</strong> Declare a serialized private int field named <code>damage</code> with a default value of <code>1</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Trigger Callback:</strong> Declare <code>private void OnTriggerEnter2D(Collider2D other)</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Target Filter:</strong> Verify that <code>other.CompareTag("Player")</code> is true.</li>
            <li><strong>Deliver Negative Health:</strong> If matched, check that <code>StatManager.manager != null</code> and call <code>StatManager.manager.ChangeHealth(-damage);</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Impact Cleanup:</strong> Call <code>Destroy(gameObject);</code> so the bullet vanishes upon contact with the player.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <p>
        Save and test in Unity. Walk into a bullet: your health drops from 3 to 2, the bullet vanishes,
        and taking 3 hits triggers the death loop and resets to Start!
    </p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — The Difficulty Matrix.</strong>
    Game difficulty is not arbitrary; it is an equation controlled by four numbers you own:</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Knob (Component)</th><th>Default</th><th>Player Experience</th></tr>
        <tr><td><code>maxAttackRange</code> (Turret)</td><td>7</td><td>How much of the map is a danger zone</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>attackCooldown</code> (Turret)</td><td>2s</td><td>How much time the player gets between dodges</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>speed</code> (Projectile)</td><td>6</td><td>How much human reaction time is required</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>damage</code> (Projectile)</td><td>1</td><td>How punishing each mistake is (out of 3 health)</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — Build Three Turret Personalities.</strong>
    Duplicate the Turret in your Overworld scene (Ctrl+D) to create three distinct challenges:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>The Sniper:</strong> Huge range (<code>12</code>), long cooldown (<code>3.5s</code>), fast bullet (<code>14</code>), high damage (<code>2</code>).</li>
        <li><strong>The Sentry:</strong> Short range (<code>4</code>), rapid cooldown (<code>0.8s</code>), slow bullet (<code>4</code>), low damage (<code>1</code>).</li>
        <li><strong>The Mortar:</strong> Medium range (<code>7</code>), balanced cooldown (<code>2s</code>), huge slow projectile (<code>3</code>), fatal damage (<code>3</code>).</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The Unfair Encounter</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>What happens if a turret has <code>speed = 25</code> and <code>attackCooldown = 0.2s</code>? What is the technical difference between an encounter that is "hard" and one that is "statistically impossible"?</li>
            <li>If you place a Sniper turret directly at the scene entrance where the player spawns, why does that violate player trust?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Playtest</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Swap seats with a partner and run a silent playtest (<a href="2.4a.html">2.4a</a> rules).
        Observe their run through your Overworld without speaking:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>Did they notice the turret before it fired, or was the first hit a surprise?</li>
        <li>Did they try to dodge bullets, or did they simply sprint through damage?</li>
        <li>Which of your three turret personalities felt the most satisfying to evade?</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Close the Loop</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>From your partner's feedback, pick <strong>one single knob</strong> to adjust (e.g. decrease projectile speed from 14 to 11). Retest. Did the play experience immediately improve?</li>
            <li>Commit and push your work: <code>"3.4a: turret damage, personalities &amp; playtest"</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Implement <code>OnTriggerEnter2D</code> on projectiles to deliver damage to the player</li>
            <li>Explain how projectile collision reuses the damage and pickup patterns from Unit 2</li>
            <li>Identify the four parameters that define turret encounter difficulty</li>
            <li>Tune multiple turret archetypes using serialized Inspector overrides</li>
            <li>Conduct a partner playtest and make an evidence-based design revision</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

